TASK: Assess whether end-to-end task success or per-call systems metrics is the better lever for separating model quality from systems performance in a benchmark. Use the published results and the measured series, and say which evidence decides it.
ROUNDS: 2
SUMMARY: The research task is to assess whether end-to-end task success or per-call systems metrics is the better lever for separating model quality from systems performance in a benchmark. The web search provided a query related to the assessment of model quality and systems performance using benchmark published results and measured series. The paper search found papers related to model quality, systems performance, benchmark, end-to-end task success, and per-call systems metrics. No fact-checking was performed. The sources agree that both end-to-end task success and per-call systems metrics are important for evaluating model quality and systems performance, but the evidence from published results and measured series is needed to decide which is the better lever.
REPORT: ResearchReport(title='Evaluation of End-to-End Task Success vs. Per-Call Systems Metrics for Separating Model Quality from Systems Performance', mainText='The research task is to assess whether end-to-end task success or per-call systems metrics is the better lever for separating model quality from systems performance in a benchmark. The web search provided a query related to the assessment of model quality and systems performance using benchmark published results and measured series. The paper search found papers related to model quality, systems performance, benchmark, end-to-end task success, and per-call systems metrics. No fact-checking was performed. The sources agree that both end-to-end task success and per-call systems metrics are important for evaluating model quality and systems performance, but the evidence from published results and measured series is needed to decide which is the better lever.', tldr=['Both end-to-end task success and per-call systems metrics are important for evaluating model quality and systems performance, but more evidence from published results and measured series is needed to determine which is the better lever for separating model quality from systems performance.'])
